     <!--#include virtual="/php/suttasidebar.html" --> 

  <div id="sutta">
  
            <h4 id="StNp4.6" class="h1">4:6  Old Age</h4>
            <div class="verse">
              <p>How short this life!</p>
              <p>You die this side of a century,</p>
              <p>but even if you live past,</p>
              <p class="v1">you die of old age.</p>
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p>People grieve</p>
                <p>for what they see as <em>mine,</em></p>
                <p>for          nothing possessed is constant,</p>
                <p class="v1">nothing is constantly possessed.<span class="fn"><a href="#stnp_note4.6.01">1</a></span><!--end fn cite--></p>
                <p>Seeing this separation</p>
                <p class="v1">simply as it is,</p>
                <p>one shouldn’t follow the household life.</p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p>At death a person abandons</p>
                <p>what he supposes as <em>mine.</em></p>
                <p>Realizing this, the wise</p>
                <p>shouldn’t incline</p>
                <p>to be devoted to <em>mine-ness.</em></p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p>Just as a man doesn’t see</p>
                <p class="v1">on awakening</p>
                <p>what he met in a dream,</p>
                <p>even so he doesn’t see,</p>
                <p>when they are dead</p>
                <p class="v1">—their time done—</p>
                <p>those he held dear.</p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p>When they are seen &amp; heard,</p>
                <p>people are called by this name or that,</p>
                <p>but only the name remains</p>
                <p>to be pointed to</p>
                <p class="v1">when they are dead.</p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p>Grief, lamentation, &amp; selfishness</p>
                <p class="v1">are not let go</p>
                <p class="v1">by those greedy for <em>mine,</em></p>
                <p>so sages</p>
                <p class="v1">letting go of possessions,</p>
                <p>go about</p>
                <p class="v1">seeing the Secure.</p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p>A monk, living withdrawn,</p>
                <p>enjoying a dwelling secluded:</p>
                <p class="v1">They say it’s congenial for him,</p>
                <p class="v1">he who wouldn’t, in any realm,</p>
                <p class="v3">display self.</p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p>Everywhere</p>
                <p class="v1">the sage</p>
                <p class="v3">independent</p>
                <p>makes nothing dear or undear.</p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p class="v1">In him</p>
                <p>lamentation &amp; selfishness,</p>
                <p>like water on a white lotus,</p>
                <p class="v1">do not adhere.</p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p>As a water bead on a lotus leaf,</p>
                <p>as water on a red lily,</p>
                <p class="v1">doesn’t adhere,</p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p class="v1">so the sage</p>
                <p class="v1">doesn’t adhere</p>
                <p>to the seen, the heard, or the sensed;</p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p class="v1">for, cleansed,</p>
                <p class="v1">he doesn’t suppose</p>
                <p class="v1">in connection</p>
                <p>with the seen, the heard, or the sensed.</p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p class="v1">In no other way</p>
                <p>does he wish for purity,</p>
                <p>for he neither takes on passion</p>
                <p class="v1">nor puts it away.<span class="fn"><a href="#stnp_note4.6.02">2</a></span><!--end fn cite--></p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
            </div><!--end verse-->
            <p class="suttaCite">vv. 804–813</p><!--end suttaCite-->
            <div class="note">
              <p class="notetitle">Notes</p><!--end notetitle-->
              <p id="stnp_note4.6.01">1. “Nothing possessed is constant, nothing is constantly possessed”—two readings of the phrase, <em>na hi santi niccā pariggahā.</em></p>
              <p id="stnp_note4.6.02">2. Nd I: An arahant has put passion totally away once and for all, and so has no need to do it ever again.</p>
            </div><!--end note-->
            <p class="seealso">See also: <a href="../../SN/SN21_2.html">SN 21:2</a>; <a href="../../AN/AN4_184.html">AN 4:184</a>; <a href="../Dhp/Ch02.html#dhp021">Dhp 21</a>; <a href="StNp5_16.html">Sn 5:16</a></p>
      </div><!--end:sutta-->
    </div><!--end:content-->
    
